content:
The five pieces are the \(U\), cross, \(I\), \(V\), and \(P\) pentominoes.

For each candidate, consider every translation of all its distinct rotations and reflections. After making a legal placement, check the uncovered part of the board for each of the other four shapes.

The placements can be checked using these necessary patterns:

- \(A\): a \(2\times3\) rectangle with the middle square of one long side missing;
- \(C\): five consecutive squares in a row or column;
- \(D\): two perpendicular arms of length \(3\);
- \(E\): a \(2\times3\) rectangle with a corner missing.

The results are:

\[
\begin{array}{c|c}
\text{Piece placed} & \text{Can another candidate still fit?}\\ \hline
A & \text{Yes}\\
B & \text{No}\\
C & \text{Yes}\\
D & \text{Yes}\\
E & \text{Yes}
\end{array}
\]

Thus, when the cross-shaped pentomino \(B\) is placed in the appropriate position, the remaining uncovered squares contain none of the patterns required by \(A,C,D,\) or \(E\). Every legal placement of each of the other four choices leaves room for at least one of the remaining shapes.

Therefore, the required shape is

\[
\boxed{\mathrm{B}}
\]
